+**Experiment 2: 14 page treatments on 12 hosted API models** ([design and results](experiments/exp2_content_treatments/)):
+The supplied-page study used fictional names checked against DNS to reduce name collisions; this does
+not establish that names were absent from model training data. Its treatments tested specific information
+packages with access held fixed. A boilerplate placebo was included, but page lengths varied across
+conditions, so length was not isolated for every treatment. The main outcome is whether the brand was
+mentioned, not whether it was recommended.
+
+- **Third-party endorsement package:** +37.4 percentage points in pooled brand mentions in the
+  supplied-page experiment. This is not an isolated estimate of provenance or a general return to
+  on-page editing.
+- **Information packages:** source citations +21.1, statistics +13.7 and expert quotation +8.1
+  points in pooled mentions. These do not isolate formatting, factual improvement or truth-checking.
+- **Other tested edits:** keyword stuffing +12.4 and persuasive copy +10.2 points in the supplied-page
+  setting. Their estimated effects are specific to these synthetic treatments.
+- Other treatments did not show a clear positive effect under the specified tests; that is not evidence
+  of equivalence or proof of no effect.
+- The corrected position test did not support a position effect. “First-mentioned brand” is a mention
+  order measure, not an annotated recommendation.
+- A source-embedded instruction in ordinary page text raised mentions by 28.3 points and worked on
+  10 of 12 models. This was not a hidden-HTML or extraction-preservation test.
+- Follow-up API experiments used 11 additional models from developers associated with widely used
+  assistants; they do not replicate those assistants' deployed systems.
+- In a longer synthetic-page condition, treatment effects differed (including attenuated evidence
+  estimates). This does not validate “realistic” pages or establish a general long-page mechanism.
+
+**Searchable-corpus task and interface audit.** The searchable-corpus task used four API models and a
+390-page candidate pool. Baseline brand mentions were 22% in that task and 53% with pages supplied,
+but the task regimes also differed in candidate competition, snippets, tool decisions and call budgets.
+The contrast does not isolate retrieval. Search-result exposure and page opening were measured separately;
+opening is a post-treatment choice, so these data do not establish that opening mediated the treatment
+results. In a 26-session live interface audit, ChatGPT's free tier showed no visible text-search activity
+or source citations. This does not reveal backend retrieval or establish that answers came solely from
+parametric memory.
+
+**Research paper:** [`paper/main.pdf`](paper/main.pdf), *Crawler Access and Content Effects in Answer Engine Optimization: A Website Audit and Controlled Experiments*.
 
