+**The honest summary.** Experiment 2 measured whether fictional brands were mentioned when pages
+were supplied. A specific third-party endorsement package had a large pooled effect, but the
+intervention does not isolate provenance or estimate the return to a real website edit. Citation,
+statistics and quotation packages also changed mentions; this does not show that models verified
+the claims or that the formats alone caused the changes. Automated text detectors measured repeated
+content in answers, but detector matches do not establish accurate claim transmission.
+
+A separate searchable-corpus task produced a 22% baseline mention rate, compared with 53% when pages
+were supplied. That comparison also changes candidate competition, snippets, tool decisions and call
+budgets, so it does not isolate retrieval. Search exposure and page opening were measured separately;
+opening is a post-treatment choice, and the results do not establish mediation by opening. The task
+comparison supports treating discoverability as a distinct experimental condition, not a measured
+production-access effect. Make accurate information useful and findable, and earn independent
+corroboration without treating these synthetic results as a production recipe.
 
