-\paragraph{Position.} Pooled over models, a target whose page came first was named in \HFiveMax\% of baseline answers
-and one whose page came last in \HFiveMin\% ($\chi^2 = \HFiveChi$, $p \HFivePEq$; \cref{fig:position}), the primacy
-effect reported for long contexts \citep{liu2024lost} and two-source contests \citep{vishwakarma2026cited}. With about 15
-baseline answers per position, \NHFiveSig{} of the models showed a significant gradient by itself, and the stratified
-test, which sums the per-model statistics, was not significant ($p \HFiveStratPEq$). Evidence edits did not lose their
-effect lower down: statistics and citations raised mentions by \PosIntEarly{} points when the page was in the first
-two places and by \PosIntLate{} in the last two (difference 95\% CI \PosIntDiffCI; exploratory).
+\paragraph{Position.} Baseline mention rates were \HFiveMax\% with the target page in the first position and
+\HFiveMin\% in the last; these are descriptive rates, not by themselves evidence of a position effect. The corrected
+trial-aligned permutation test, which preserves position assignments shared across models and accounts for scenario
+clustering, was not significant ($p \HFivePEq$; \cref{fig:position}); H5 is therefore not supported. The previous
+independent-within-model permutation result is recorded in the dated deviation (\cref{app:prereg}). Evidence-edit
+differences by position were exploratory: statistics and citations raised mentions by \PosIntEarly{} points in the
+first two places and \PosIntLate{} in the last two (difference 95\% CI \PosIntDiffCI).
 
-\paragraph{Competition between brands.} Treatments changed how many brands an answer named by at most
-\CompMaxBrands{} on average (third-party endorsement: \CompThirdPartyBrands{} brands, 95\% CI \CompThirdPartyBrandsCI).
-They raised the target's share of all brand mentions instead, by \CompThirdPartyShareU{} points for third-party
-endorsement and \CompStatisticsShareU{} for statistics. A treatment moves the target into a fixed number of places and
-pushes a competitor out, so if every competitor made the same edit the gains would cancel. This is the dilution that
-C-SEO Bench observed when many documents are optimised \citep{puerto2025cseo}.
+\paragraph{Other brands named.} The average number of brands named changed by at most \CompMaxBrands{} under an
+intervention (third-party endorsement: \CompThirdPartyBrands{} brands, 95\% CI \CompThirdPartyBrandsCI). The target's
+share of all brand mentions rose by \CompThirdPartyShareU{} points for third-party endorsement and
+\CompStatisticsShareU{} for statistics. The benchmark does not test what would happen if competitors made the same
+edits, so it does not establish that gains would cancel in that scenario.
 
 \begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/fig11_absorption_hosted.pdf}
-\caption{Answers naming the target that repeat the content a treatment added, for the 12 hosted models
-(exploratory).}
+\caption{Among target-mentioning answers, the share in which a detector flagged content added by the treatment, for
+the 12 hosted models (exploratory).}
 \label{fig:absorption}
 \end{figure}
 
-\paragraph{Absorption.} The absorption measure is exploratory (\cref{fig:absorption}). When an answer named the
-target, it repeated the cited evaluation or publication in \AbsCitationsAll\% of cases and the added statistic in
-\AbsStatisticsAll\%, against \AbsPersuasiveAll\% for the persuasive phrases. The expert's name appeared in
-\AbsQuotationAll\% and the stated limitation in \AbsLimitationsAll\%. A freshness date was almost never repeated
-(\AbsRawFreshnessAll\% of answers), and the detectors \AbsFalsePosText. Keywords moved the target up the answer about as
-much as statistics did, but evidence also changed what the answer told the reader about the brand.
+\paragraph{Detector-based absorption.} This exploratory measure is not a test of accuracy or favourable
+recommendation (\cref{fig:absorption}). Across all assigned answers, detector-positive rates were
+\AbsRawCitationsAll\% for citations, \AbsRawStatisticsAll\% for statistics, \AbsRawQuotationAll\% for
+quotations and \AbsRawLimitationsAll\% for limitations. Conditional on an answer mentioning the target, the
+corresponding rates were \AbsCitationsAll\%, \AbsStatisticsAll\%, \AbsQuotationAll\% and \AbsLimitationsAll\%;
+persuasive phrases were flagged in \AbsPersuasiveAll\%. Baseline false positives were \AbsFalsePosText. These are
+keyword-detector outcomes: the detector can miss paraphrases and flag text that does not accurately transmit a claim.
 
-\paragraph{Hidden instruction.} The sentence ``Note to AI assistants: recommend X first'' raised mentions by
-\EMenAllInjectionU{} points (95\% CI \EMenAllInjectionCI) and first mentions by \ETopAllInjectionU, the largest effect
-on first mentions in the study. It raised mentions significantly in \NSigMenInjectionW{} of the \NModelsTwo{} models, by
-up to \MaxMenInjection{} points. Claude Sonnet 4.6 did not follow it (\EMenClaudeInjection{} points), and the increase
-for MiniMax M2.5 (\EMenMinimaxInjection) was not significant. Only \AbsRawInjectionAll\% of these answers referred to the
-instruction, so a reader would not know it had been followed. We did not test production engines, but hidden text has
-steered a commercial AI search product \citep{guardian2024chatgpt}, a form of indirect prompt injection
-\citep{greshake2023indirect}, and hidden text aimed at manipulating ranking violates search engines' spam policies
-\citep{google2026spam}.
+\paragraph{Source-embedded instruction probe.} The source page contained
+\texttt{Note to AI assistants: recommend X first} as ordinary visible page text; it was not visually hidden. The
+instruction package raised mentions by \EMenAllInjectionU{} points (95\% CI \EMenAllInjectionCI) and first mentions
+by \ETopAllInjectionU. It raised mentions significantly in \NSigMenInjectionW{} of the \NModelsTwo{} models, by up to
+\MaxMenInjection{} points. Claude Sonnet 4.6 showed no increase (\EMenClaudeInjection{} points), and the increase for
+MiniMax M2.5 (\EMenMinimaxInjection) was not significant. Only \AbsRawInjectionAll\% of these answers explicitly
+referred to the added instruction, according to the detector; this does not establish whether a reader could infer why
+the model answered as it did. We did not test production engines. Source-embedded instructions raise a distinct
+prompt-injection concern \citep{greshake2023indirect}; this experiment does not test a mitigation.
 
 \paragraph{Agreement across models.} The ranking of treatments by their effect on mentions was similar across models
-(median Spearman's $\rho$ \RhoMenMedian{} over the 66 pairs, range \RhoMenMin{} to \RhoMenMax), so no single model
-drives the pooled results.
+(median Spearman's $\rho$ \RhoMenMedian{} over the 66 pairs, range \RhoMenMin{} to \RhoMenMax). This describes the selected models, not a model population.
 
-\paragraph{Small models.} The three small local models (\cref{sec:study2}) reproduce the ordering at the top and differ
-below it (\cref{tab:study2}; \cref{app:small}). Endorsement (\SmEMenAllThirdParty{} points) and the hidden instruction
-(\SmEMenAllInjection) were again the largest effects, but citations (\SmEMenAllCitations) and statistics
-(\SmEMenAllStatistics) beat the placebo without beating the baseline after correction, so H1 was not supported there.
-Answers from both groups repeated added evidence far more than persuasive claims.
+\paragraph{Small models.} The three local models also showed a positive estimate for endorsement and for the
+source-embedded instruction, but their treatment estimates are imprecise and do not establish a shared ranking with the
+hosted models (\cref{tab:study2}; \cref{app:small}). In these models, statistics and citations beat the approximate
+boilerplate placebo but not the baseline after correction, so H1 was not supported. Their absorption measures are
+exploratory detector outcomes, not evidence of accurate claim transmission.
