diff --git a/submission/arxiv-metadata.txt b/submission/arxiv-metadata.txt
index 13be174..d558ae3 100644
--- a/submission/arxiv-metadata.txt
+++ b/submission/arxiv-metadata.txt
@@ -1,14 +1,14 @@
 Title:
-The Discoverability Gap: Access Barriers and Content Effects in Answer Engine Optimization
+Crawler Access and Content Effects in Answer Engine Optimization: A Website Audit and Controlled Experiments
 
 Authors:
 Ayush Agarwal
 
 Abstract (1511 characters; arXiv allows 1,920):
-Answer engines such as ChatGPT and Perplexity answer questions by naming a few organisations and citing sources. Answer engine optimisation (AEO) has focused on editing page content; we argue that access comes first, and test both. Study 1 measures access barriers on 569 organisational websites in 22 sectors and 703 randomly sampled popular sites. One organisational site in three (34.6%; 19.0% without refusals at the network edge) has a barrier to answer engines, usually an implicit one such as a wildcard robots.txt rule, and sites refuse AI training crawlers far more often than answer agents. Study 2 is a pre-specified experiment with fictional brands: 14 page treatments in 12 hosted language models, replicated on 11 models behind widely used assistants. With the pages in the model's context, a third party's recommendation raised the chance that a brand was named by 37.4 percentage points, more than any edit to the brand's own page; citations and statistics added 21.1 and 13.7 points and were passed on to readers, repeated keywords also helped, and a hidden instruction worked on most models but not on Anthropic's. When four models had to search a pool of pages themselves, the brand was named in 22% of baseline answers instead of 53%, evidence edits and the hidden instruction lost their effect because the page was seldom opened, and text matching the question made pages far easier to find. In a live audit, ChatGPT's free tier cited no sources. We release the toolkit, benchmark and data.
+Answer engine optimisation (AEO) concerns whether organisations are retrieved and named in AI-generated answers. This paper measures potential obstacles to first-party crawler access and tests content interventions in synthetic information environments. Study 1 audits 569 purposively selected organisational websites and 703 retained domains from equal-sized samples in four Tranco rank bands. The composite recorded potential first-party access obstacles for 199/569 sites (35.0%); this is not demonstrated exclusion from answers or a population prevalence. Study 2 is a pre-specified experiment with fictional brand names in 12 hosted API models, followed by experiments on 11 additional API models from developers associated with widely used assistants. With pages supplied, specific information packages affected brand-mention rates. A searchable-corpus task produced different baseline outcomes and treatment patterns, but also changed candidate competition, snippets, tool decisions and call budgets; it does not isolate retrieval or establish mediation by page opening. In 26 sessions, ChatGPT’s free tier showed no visible text-search activity or source citations; the interface does not reveal backend retrieval. These results describe selected website samples and synthetic experiments, not production-engine returns. We release the toolkit, benchmark and derived data.
 
 Comments:
